# 0. Simple web stack

![Simple one-server web infrastructure](./0-simple_web_stack_whiteboard.png)

## Explanation, starting from the user

A user opens their browser and types `www.foobar.com` to reach the website. Before the browser can send any request, it has to turn that name into an IP address, so it queries the DNS system. The domain `foobar.com` has a `www` record configured to point to the server's IP, `8.8.8.8`. DNS returns that IP, and the browser then sends an HTTP request directly to `8.8.8.8`.

On that single server, the request first hits the **web server (Nginx)**. Nginx handles the raw HTTP request and either serves it directly (for static files) or forwards it to the **application server**. The application server runs the **application files** (the code base) — the actual logic of the website — and, when it needs data, queries the **database (MySQL)**. The application server builds a response using that data and hands it back through Nginx, which sends it over the network to the user's browser, which renders the page.

## Specifics about this infrastructure

**What is a server?**
A server is a physical or virtual machine — with its own CPU, RAM, storage, and operating system — that is connected to a network and runs software (like a web server or database) to respond to requests from other computers (clients).

**What is the role of the domain name?**
A domain name (`foobar.com`) gives the website a human-readable, memorable name instead of requiring users to remember and type a raw IP address. It also lets the owner point that name to a different server or IP later without users needing to change anything.

**What type of DNS record is `www` in `www.foobar.com`?**
It is an **A record** (or in some configurations a CNAME record pointing to another name) — a record that maps the subdomain `www.foobar.com` to the server's IP address, `8.8.8.8`.

**What is the role of the web server?**
Nginx receives incoming HTTP requests, serves static content (HTML, CSS, JS, images) directly, and passes dynamic requests on to the application server. It's the front door of the infrastructure.

**What is the role of the application server?**
The application server executes the application's business logic — it runs the code base, processes the request, interacts with the database as needed, and generates the dynamic content that gets sent back to the user.

**What is the role of the database?**
MySQL stores and retrieves the website's persistent data (users, posts, orders, etc.). The application server reads from and writes to the database to build responses and save information.

**What does the server use to communicate with the user's computer?**
The server and the user's browser communicate over the network using the **HTTP** (or HTTPS) protocol, both built on top of TCP/IP.

## Issues with this infrastructure

**SPOF (Single Point of Failure)**
Every component — Nginx, the application server, the code, and the database — runs on one single server. If that server fails (hardware failure, crash, network outage), the entire website goes down with no fallback.

**Downtime when maintenance is needed**
Because everything shares the same machine, routine maintenance causes an outage. For example, deploying new code or updating the web server's configuration requires restarting Nginx, which drops incoming connections and takes the site offline, even if only briefly.

**Cannot scale if traffic increases**
A single server has a fixed amount of CPU, RAM, and network capacity. Once traffic exceeds what that machine can handle, there is no second server to share the load — the only option is scaling *up* (a bigger machine), which has a hard ceiling, rather than scaling *out* (adding more machines).
